From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps4-gladius <ps4-gladius@invalid>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: drain the in-process wake pipe with poll.

The main loop empties its wake pipe with

    while (read( get_unix_fd( fd ), buffer, sizeof(buffer) ) > 0);

which needs a non-blocking descriptor to end. This console has none: it refuses
both fcntl( F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK ) and ioctl FIONBIO (see 0742), so that loop
reads what the wake-up left and then waits, inside the server thread, for a byte
nobody will send. The first timeout to arrive would have hung the server for
good -- it has not bitten yet only because nothing has run long enough to need
a timer.

Ask poll what is there instead. Every other caller treats non-blocking mode as
an optimisation; this is the one that depended on it for correctness.
---
diff --git a/server/request.c b/server/request.c
--- a/server/request.c
+++ b/server/request.c
@@ -915,7 +915,20 @@ static void inprocess_wake_poll_event( struct fd *fd, int event )
 {
     char buffer[64];
 
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* ⚠ This descriptor is blocking and cannot be made otherwise (see
+     * pw_set_nonblocking), so the loop below would not end: it reads what the
+     * wake-up left, then waits inside the server thread for a byte nobody will
+     * send. Read only what poll reports. */
+    {
+        struct pollfd wake = { get_unix_fd( fd ), POLLIN, 0 };
+
+        while (poll( &wake, 1, 0 ) > 0 && (wake.revents & POLLIN))
+            if (read( wake.fd, buffer, sizeof(buffer) ) <= 0) break;
+    }
+#else
     while (read( get_unix_fd( fd ), buffer, sizeof(buffer) ) > 0);
+#endif
 }
 
 static const struct fd_ops inprocess_wake_fd_ops =
